y pushing kids out who are  difficult to teach I see no evidence  that they are on the other hand I see no  evidence that they're not if these  stories are true about you know got to  go less or your counseling it's just a  lot more subtle than be open but do they  call into question Success Academy or  other charter schools success are you a  fan of success or not I would argue not  like I've gotten myself in a little bit  of trouble with this because I  personally and I'm speaking just for  myself have no problem at all with  charter schools functioning as what I  would describe is a poor man's private  school you know full disclosure I was a  public school teacher I send my daughter  to a private school I went to school in  a decent suburban school where we never  had to deal with problems of the hardest  to teach of the disruptive kid where do  these kids go we say are we to believe  that they don't exist in in better zip  codes  one of the things and again just  speaking for myself that kind of bothers  me about this argument in general is why  we only upset let's just assume for a  second that it's true okay so and I'd  again have no reason to believe that it  is but let's just say the worst  allegations are true  she is counseling out the hardest to  teach she's creating this poor man's  private school why is that a bad thing  in other words are we are we saying that  if you are a poor black and brown kid  it's a problem that you should have what  a what an affluent kid in the Upper East  Side has a disruptive free studious high  quality school why is that unfair well  let those kids talk about that because  the education historian and arguably the  most prominent charter school critic  around Diane Ravich read the following  in response to this controversy about  Success Academy and I quote public  schools are supposed to work for all  kids granted there are magnet schools  and special schools men there are  supposed to be public schools where no  one has turned away  no one has canceled out nameless so okay  so that's name one a few months ago I  wrote a column for US news about this  and I wrote about a kid who had been  facing what seemed to be the exact same  circumstances that the New York Times  highlighted for some of these that the  the got to go kids and it turns out the  kid was in the leafy suburban community  of Irvington New York and suddenly was  his parents felt harassed and they ended  up moving to it to a school system  nearby where he was he was able to be  taken care of another data point that  came up in this piece  I think was Montclair New Jersey is  spending something like five million  dollars a year on out of district  placements for 70 some-odd kids now I  you know because of privacy rules it's  impossible to know the circumstance of  each of those kids do they have a  condition that really can't be met by  the the Montclair school system but the  bottom line is kids in Montclair don't  have to deal with the with the  disruptive kids etcetera yeah in the US  News column that you read the subhead is  why is it unfair to give poor families  the studious disruption free schools the  rich take for granted that zigzag right  so I'm not taking a side here I want to  be clear on that but I just want to be  clear eyed about it I just don't buy  this idea that that public schools take  everybody  you know then this raises a question you  know a charter network that with the  size and resources of Success Academy  shouldn't they have the capacity to  cater to all children without dumping  you know problem students back into the  day that's what they're doing yeah I  think I think that's a fair thing to say  and and let me put in a plug for  democracy prep where I work we've  actually created a program called  pathways where we educate special ed  kids from throughout the network in a  single what we would call it district 75  so the situation in New York City so  what do you mean by the district 75 is a  special education district within the  New York City school system one of the  criticisms and charters is though you  don't work with kids with with IEP s and  special education well that's not true  of all of us some of us do yeah but  again I want to be clear about this the  spirit of what charters do that should  be their choice I would argue in other  words should as soon as you duplicate  the conditions of the public school  system you're going to duplicate their  results so while on the one hand I'm not  very fond of charter advocates saying  things like oh we take all the same  children we get all the we get better  results yeah you have a different set of  rules let's be candid about that having  said that what I want to impose a set of  rules on success not necessarily I think  it's important to let charter schools do  what charter schools do well having said  that I think a lot of the conflict is  when there is this we say one thing and  we do another look the other issue in a  sense our charters may be fulfilling a  role that  parochial schools used to you know so  it's like you scratch you know I mean  parochial schools in most cities started  losing population around 1960 when  populations been that but they were  cheap relative to private schools  private academies and you would see you  know ethnics well you know black and  white ethnic sending their kids have  regardless of religion yeah and I think  if we're fair about this I think a lot  of the so-called high-performing  no-excuses charter schools are a  cut-rate version of what Catholic  schools the role that they used to play  in our inner cities they're good I think  I'm not sure they're as good as those  those Catholic schools are or used to be  but look the other issue that we're not  talking about here is is the issue of  school tone so whether whether or not a  kid is meet the test for giftedness  almost doesn't matter  you have to create the conditions that  allow productive learning to go on you  have to valorize normalize you know a  studious environment if you've ever been  to a Success Academy they do that  exceptionally well and I say this having  been a fifth grade publican teacher for  many many years I could have gotten so  much more done if I didn't spend all of  my time not just managing three or four  disruptive kids but having to frankly  cater to them and ignoring the rest all  right well we will leave it there thank  you so much we've been talking with  Robert Pandey CEO he's the vice  president at the education think-tank  the Thomas Fordham foundation and a  former public school teacher in the  Bronx Robert thanks for talking thanks  for having me for reason TV I'm Nick  Gillespie
 

